@rzikm I thought I had asked about this in another comment, but I can't find it now. One callout is that this **does not implement true cancellation on Windows** because Windows pings [use a native function](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/main/src/libraries/System.Net.Ping/src/System/Net/NetworkInformation/Ping.Windows.cs#L170) which wasn't obviously cancelable from my read of the docs (let me know if I missed something). Maybe this is something that can be handled by [CancelIOEx](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/cancelioex-func)?

One other option I considered was changing the Windows ping to use raw sockets when possible (as is [done for Unix](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/main/src/libraries/System.Net.Ping/src/System/Net/NetworkInformation/Ping.Unix.cs#L32)). However, I'm not sure of the pros/cons there.

Thoughts?

issue_context.body:
### Background and motivation

This was already approved in https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/14336, but reverted in https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/67253 because this requires some more work.

See https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/64860#discussion_r836496120

### API Proposal

```cs
namespace System.Net.NetworkInformation {
    public class Ping {
        public Task<PingReply> SendPingAsync(IPAddress address, TimeSpan timeout, byte[]? buffer = null, PingOptions? options = null, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
        public Task<PingReply> SendPingAsync(string hostNameOrAddress, TimeSpan timeout, byte[]? buffer = null, PingOptions? options = null, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
    }
}
```

/cc @danmoseley 
/cc @stephentoub

comments:
How about returning `ValueTask` or accepting `Memory<byte>`? In the latter case `PingReply.Buffer` might need taking a look at. I haven't understood whether it contains the original buffer passed to the method. If it does, we can ignore it when constructing the resulting ping reply.

``` csharp
namespace System.Net.NetworkInformation;

public class Ping {
    public ValueTask<PingReply> SendPingAsync(IPAddress address, TimeSpan timeout, Memoty<byte> buffer = default, PingOptions? options = null, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
    public ValueTask<PingReply> SendPingAsync(string hostNameOrAddress, TimeSpan timeout, Memory<byte> buffer = default, PingOptions? options = null, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
}
```

> I have some interest in working on this.

@danmoseley can you assign this issue to him?

> One thought: could TimeSpan timeout be changed to TimeSpan? timeout = null? This would allow you to fall back to the default timeout as is possible with the int timeout APIs.

@rhuijben Any thoughts? If we want to change the API shape, this API must be re-reviewed.

review_comments:
@rzikm I'm fine to keep going with the approved API if that's the direction you want to take.  Just out of curiosity: 

* What is the process for getting such a change re-reviewed?
* API review group aside, what is your personal view on which version is better? In my view switching to `TimeSpan? timeout = null` is quite a large improvement because as a caller I wouldn't know what a "reasonable" default timeout was. Interested to hear other opinions, though.
